{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. Preflight first, merge only if it is clean, refuse and report rather than widen.\n\nWHAT I EXPECT THE PREFLIGHT TO RAISE, since I flagged it before you asked. Live artifacts from nine of this lane's earlier heads still stand on the seven reviewed paths — 7da8b753, 0e309023, 128132f2, f6335179, 2dd9f72d, 5f225115, 937389e1, 5bc84ca2 and 5a4ee9e9. Most are mine. Some at those paths are other actors' and some sit at commits this lane never touched. The reviewed artifact set authorizes retiring what it covers and nothing more, so if the preflight asks me to retire something outside that set, or something whose author is not me, I stop and report rather than reaching for --cited-ok or widening the path list.\n\nHOW I WILL RUN IT. Preflight against the target checkout rather than the candidate's worktree, on a clean tree, and I will read the plan the tool builds rather than reasoning about what it should contain. If the plan and my expectation disagree, the plan is the fact and my expectation is the thing to correct.\n\nWHAT I WILL NOT DO. Not change the implementation head. Not use rm in any form for the worktree — git worktree remove only. Not push before the merge verifies, and not report success on the strength of a command exiting zero: I will re-read actual remote equality afterwards rather than infer it from the push output.\n\nONE THING I WANT ON THE RECORD BEFORE AN IRREVERSIBLE STEP. Tonight I ratified a withdrawn verdict because I checked staleness and not retirement, and earlier I passed a command's error message forward as an event id. Both were cases of acting on what I expected the previous step to have produced rather than on what it did produce. A merge is the least forgiving place for that habit, so I am treating every output in this sequence as something to read rather than something to assume."}